course: myths
lesson: 29
title: Let's test it! Experiments and fair tests
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ava's gran chats to her tomatoes every morning and swears they grow faster for it. Ava doesn't sneer; she sets up two seed trays instead.
explain: A fair test changes only ONE thing: same seeds, same soil, same water, same window, and only one tray gets talked to. If you change two things, you can't tell which one made the difference.
explain: Then you count and measure, because memory is a liar: you remember the toast that landed butter down and forget the ones that didn't.
explain: Repeat it many times, write the results down, and be ready for three honest endings: "the saying is wrong", "it's partly right", or "even scientists aren't sure yet".
explain: Some tests are for grown-ups only, like anything hot; the rule is, if it's hot, sharp or electric, a grown-up runs it.
fact: Studies on talking to plants are mixed; any effect might come from breath, attention or extra care, so it is not proven.
fact: The idea that hot water can freeze faster than cold is called the Mpemba effect, after a Tanzanian schoolboy who noticed it in 1963; scientists still argue about when, or whether, it really happens.
fact: Toast falling off a table often turns about half a turn before it lands, so it lands butter side down quite often from table height; a scientist tested this with lots of toast.
fact: Modern phones stop charging when the battery is full; heat harms a battery more than charging overnight.
fact: Tests found that a spoon in an open fizzy bottle makes no difference; a tight lid keeps the fizz best.
fact: A banana is a good snack, but it does not prevent cramp in sport for most people.
fact: Ordinary paper is very hard to fold in half more than about seven times, but in 2002 a student, Britney Gallivan, folded a very long thin strip 12 times.
fact: Without air in the way, heavy and light things fall at the same speed; on the Moon in 1971 an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather and they landed together; on Earth, air pushes against light, wide things like a feather and slows them, and a sheet of paper laid flat on top of a book falls with it, because the book keeps the air off it.
fact: Plants need light, water, air and nutrients (plant food from the soil); they do not need music, and studies on sound and plants are unclear.
fact: Sugar dissolves (breaks up and vanishes into the water) faster in warm water than in cold, because the tiny particles of warm water move faster.
fact: A fair test changes only one thing and keeps everything else the same.
fact: Counting many tries is better evidence than remembering a few.
exercise: In a fair test, how many things do you change at once? | Only one, and keep everything else the same | As many as you can at once, to save lots of time | Two at once, so you can compare both of them | None at all, you just watch carefully and guess
exercise: How could Ava fairly test whether talking helps plants grow? | Two identical trays, same light and water, talk to only one | Talk to one plant and compare it with a cactus | Talk to every plant and see if they grow at all | Put one tray in the sun and talk to the other
exercise: Is it proven that talking to plants makes them grow faster? | No, studies are mixed and not clear | Yes, plants understand kind words | Yes, but only if you talk loudly | No, talking to plants always kills them
exercise: What do scientists say about hot water freezing faster than cold? | They still argue; it is an open question | It always freezes faster, every time | It can never freeze faster, it's impossible | Only salty water can freeze at all
exercise: Who was the Mpemba effect named after? | A Tanzanian schoolboy who noticed it in 1963 | A Greek scientist from ancient times | An ice-cream company in Italy | A polar explorer who was stuck for months in the Arctic
exercise: Why does toast often land butter side down from a table? | It turns about half a turn on the way down | The butter makes it much heavier on that side | The floor pulls the butter towards it | Toast always lands that way from any height
exercise: How should Mia check whether bread lands butter side down? | Drop it many times and count the results | Remember the last time it happened to her | Ask all her friends what they think happens | Drop one slice just once and decide from that
exercise: Does charging a phone overnight wreck a modern battery? | No, it stops charging when it is full | Yes, it overfills and the battery swells | Yes, every night takes a year off it | Only if the phone is switched on
exercise: What harms a phone battery more than overnight charging? | Heat | Darkness | Quiet rooms | Long text messages
exercise: What keeps an open fizzy drink fizzy best? | A tight lid | A metal spoon in the neck | A wooden spoon in the neck | Shaking the bottle first
exercise: Does eating a banana before a match stop cramp? | No, it's a good snack but not a cramp cure | Yes, one banana stops all cramp | Yes, but only green bananas work | Only if you eat the skin too
exercise: Can paper only ever be folded in half seven times? | No, a student folded a long thin strip 12 times | Yes, it's a law of nature for paper | Yes, the eighth fold always tears it straight in half | No, any paper folds 50 times easily
exercise: What happened when an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon? | They landed at the same time | The hammer landed long before the feather | The feather floated away into space | The feather landed first
exercise: Why does a feather fall slower than a hammer on Earth? | Air pushes against the light, wide feather | Feathers are pulled much less strongly by gravity | Feathers are filled up with a special lighter air | Hammers are made of a metal that loves to fall
exercise: Do plants need music to grow well? | No, they need light, water, air and nutrients | Yes, they need to hear some music every single day | Only classical music helps them, never pop songs | Yes, or else they grow round and round in a spiral
exercise: Does sugar dissolve faster in cold or warm water? | Warm water | Cold water | Exactly the same in both | Sugar never dissolves in water
exercise: Why is counting better than remembering? | We remember the surprising times and forget the rest | Counting makes the results come out right | Remembering is not allowed in science | Numbers are always more exciting
test: "talk to my tomatoes every day and they're massive, so it works innit" What's the problem with this proof? | There's no untalked-to tray to compare with | Tomatoes can't be grown in pots | It proves that talking works on every single plant | The tomatoes might be the wrong colour
test: Lola puts one pot by the radio in a sunny window and one in a dark cupboard. The radio pot grows better. What can she conclude? | Nothing about music, because she changed the light too | Music makes plants grow better | Darkness makes plants hate the music, so they grow slower | Radios give out plant food
test: Ben says, "Hot water ALWAYS freezes faster, my science club proved it once." What's the honest reply? | One try isn't enough, and scientists still argue about it | He's right, one try proves it forever | He's wrong, hot water can never freeze before cold water does | Only scientists are allowed to test it
test: "bloody toast landed butter down AGAIN, it always does" What's true? | From table height it often does, because it turns about half a turn | It's pure bad luck, there's no reason at all | Toast is magnetic on the buttered side | It never really happens, you imagine it
test: Uncle Pete unplugs his phone at 80% and leaves it in a hot car all afternoon. Which habit is worse for the battery? | Leaving it in the hot car | Unplugging it when it hits 80% | Charging it at night | Turning it off sometimes
test: Leah's gran puts a spoon in the cola every time. How could they test the trick fairly? | Two opened bottles, one with a spoon, one without, checked after a day | One bottle with a spoon, checked after a minute | Ask gran whether she thinks it works | Use a spoon in lemonade and a lid on cola
test: Charlie drops a book and a flat sheet of paper; the book wins. Then he lays the paper flat on top of the book. What happens? | They fall together, because the book shields the paper from air | The paper flies off and floats away | The book falls slower because of the paper | The paper falls first and lands under the book
test: In a cartoon, a cannonball smashes the floor while a feather drifts down, and a robot says gravity pulls the cannonball harder. What should he say instead? | Air slows the feather; without air they'd fall together | He's right, heavy things are always pulled down much faster | The feather is too clever to fall | Cannonballs have their own gravity
test: Josh says, "Seven folds, max, no one can ever do more." What's the clever reply? | With ordinary paper, yes, but a student folded a long strip 12 times | You're right, eight folds would explode | Anyone can fold paper 100 times | Folding paper is impossible after two
test: Dev guesses sugar dissolves faster in cold water. What's the best thing to do? | Time a spoonful in cold and in warm water | Tell him he's silly for guessing | Agree, because guesses are usually right | Use boiling water and skip the cold one altogether
test: Ruby's coach says bananas stop cramp. What's fair to say? | Bananas are a good snack, but they don't really stop cramp | Bananas cause cramp, never eat them | The coach is lying to sell bananas | Cramp only ever happens to people who refuse to eat bananas
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a fair test. | Only one condition is varied whilst all others are held constant | All conditions are varied simultaneously | The outcome is decided before the test is conducted | A single trial is deemed sufficient
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present scientific position concerning the more rapid freezing of warm water? | The matter remains disputed among scientists | It has been conclusively proven in all circumstances | It has been conclusively disproven in all circumstances | It has never been investigated
exam: It is given that a hammer and a feather were released simultaneously upon the lunar surface in 1971. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Both objects reached the surface at the same moment | The hammer reached the surface considerably earlier | The feather reached the surface considerably earlier | The feather did not descend at all
exam: Ascertain the condition under which granulated sugar dissolves more rapidly. | In warm water | In cold water | In water devoid of motion | In frozen water
